# AGENTS.md

{{synopsis}}. The owner reviews every change.
Write code that {{reader}} can read. Setup is in `README.md`.

## Checks

`scripts/check` runs every gate: source layout, scalafmt, the build settings in `project.scala`,
a search for switched-off checks, then a build with warnings and WartRemover findings as errors,
and the tests. The git hook and CI run it too. It works without mise activated, from any folder.

- Work in small steps and keep `scripts/check` green after each one.
- Done means `scripts/check` is green. Show the command and the end of its output.
- When a check stays red after two honest attempts, stop and report what you tried.
- Fix the cause of a red check. Gate files change only with the owner's explicit approval:
  `scripts/check`, `.githooks/`, `.github/`, `.claude/`, `project.scala`, `mise.toml`,
  `mise.lock`, `.scalafmt.conf`, and existing tests.

Every command below passes `--server=false`. With the Bloop compile server, a warning that
`-Werror` turns into an error still exits 0, so a red build would look green.

```sh
# one suite
mise exec -- scala-cli test project.scala src --server=false --test-only '{{namespace}}.{{module}}Suite'
# one test by name (a munit glob)
mise exec -- scala-cli test project.scala src --server=false --test-only '{{namespace}}.{{module}}Suite' -- '*blank*'
# format
mise exec -- scala-cli fmt project.scala src
# compile only, or with a long explanation of each error
mise exec -- scala-cli compile project.scala src --server=false
mise exec -- scala-cli compile project.scala src --server=false -O -explain
# run
mise exec -- scala-cli run project.scala src --server=false -- <args>
```

Sandboxed agents: mise, Coursier and Scala CLI write to their caches (listed in
`.claude/settings.json`). A first run downloads from `repo1.maven.org`, `repo.scala-lang.org`,
`github.com` and `objects.githubusercontent.com`; `gh` needs `api.github.com`. A run with warm
caches needs no network. Without these, `scripts/check` fails for reasons outside the code.

## Scala

Read `CODING_STANDARDS.md` before writing or reviewing Scala.

The compiler and WartRemover reject these. Write the form on the right:

- A value you drop on purpose: `val _ = expr`, never for an `Either` or a `Try`: handle the
  error. An unused lambda parameter: `_ =>`. The compiler reports unused private and local
  parameters only; remove unused public ones too.
- A Java result that can be null: `Option(javaCall())`.
- `==` needs proof that the two types can be equal. Add `derives CanEqual` to enums and case
  classes. For a Java type, add one given in a shared object, such as
  `given CanEqual[Instant, Instant] = CanEqual.derived`. Never `CanEqual[Any, Any]`.
- `.head`, `.get`, `.last`, `xs(i)`, `xs.updated(i, x)` and `???` throw. Use `headOption`,
  `lift(i)`, `getOrElse`, `fold` or `match`. `throw` is banned: return an `Either`.
- Not checked, so review rejects them: `m(key)` on a `Map` (use `get`), `s.toInt` (use
  `toIntOption`), a `match` on a non-sealed type such as `Int` without a `case _`, and
  `catch case _` or `Try(...).foreach`, which drop the error.
- `var`, `while`, `return`, `null`, casts and `scala.collection.mutable` are banned. Build
  results with `map`, `foldLeft` or `++` on immutable collections.
- Text: `toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)`, and string interpolation `s"..."` instead of `"a" + value`.
- Scala 3 syntax: `*` imports, `given` and `using` instead of `implicit`. No `scala.language`
  imports.
- A pattern that may not match: write a `match` with every case, never `@unchecked`.
- Property tests go in a `munit.ScalaCheckSuite` with `property(...)`. A plain ScalaCheck
  `Properties` object never runs.

The search for switched-off checks bans some names in code: `nowarn`, `unused` and
`unchecked` after a dot, and `type X = Any` (or `AnyRef`, `AnyVal`, `Matchable`, `Object`). In
test files (a path with `test` in it) it also bans `only`, `ignore`, `fail`, `pending` and `flaky`
after a dot, `.tag(`, `TestOptions`, `withTags`, `isCI`, a method named `test` or `property`, and
munit's tags (`munit.Fail`, `import munit.*`). Name your own code differently, such as
`rejectWith` instead of `fail`. Lines that hold only a comment are not searched. Test tags are
not used: every test runs on every run.

## Layout

- All code lives in `src/` as `.scala` files. `scripts/check` fails on `.scala`, `.sc` or `.java`
  files anywhere else, on symlinks in `src/`, and on names with characters other than letters,
  digits, `_`, `.` and `-`.
- Every suite runs at least one test and ignores none. `scripts/check` reads munit's summary.
- Logic goes in `src/main/scala`. `src/main/scala/Main.scala` only calls it.
- Tests go in `src/test/scala`. Every munit suite there runs automatically.
- Build settings live only in `project.scala`: the Scala version, compiler options, WartRemover
  and dependencies, each with an exact version. A new dependency needs the owner's approval.
- `mise.toml` pins the JDK and Scala CLI. `mise.lock` holds their checksums.

## Writing

For chat replies, commits, PRs, code comments and docs. Many readers are not native English
speakers.

- Short sentences and plain words. Lead with the result. Then what the reader needs to act: what
  changed, what failed, what is left, what you did not verify.
- Say what a technical idea does before you name it.
- A chat reply fits one screen. Use a table for parallel facts and a numbered list for steps.
  Keep every risk and open question.
- Name files by repo-relative path.
- Docs state rules and pointers, never project status. Status goes stale.
- Commits and PRs describe the change only, with no tool or AI attribution.
- PR title: `type(scope): what changed`, at most 72 characters. CI checks it. PR body: fill
  `.github/pull_request_template.md`, one line per slot. One concern per PR, about 200 changed
  lines at most. Open it with `gh pr create --assignee @me`, so the owner is assigned. Stack
  bigger work as PRs whose base is the branch below. Squash merges make the PR title and body the
  commit on `main`.

## Review

Review each PR on its own. Before a stack merges, review it once as a whole for problems between
PRs only:

```sh
git diff $(git merge-base origin/main <top>)..<top>
```
